  "image_name": "5c5aa841e6f7068c80a448b0d042b8db.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the autonomic nerve supply of the heart, showing how sympathetic and vagal pathways connect the brainstem, spinal cord, peripheral ganglia, and cardiac plexus. The heart at the bottom receives a dense network of fibers from both sides of the neck and upper thorax.\n\nSympathetic pathways are shown in red. Preganglionic neurons arise from upper thoracic spinal cord levels, especially T1–T4, pass through white rami to the sympathetic chain, and then ascend or synapse in cervical and thoracic ganglia such as the stellate and superior cervical ganglia. Postganglionic cardiac nerves then project to the cardiac plexus and heart. Vagal pathways are shown in blue. They descend from the medulla through the vagus nerves, give off cervical and thoracic cardiac branches, and send fibers to the cardiac plexus and intrinsic cardiac ganglia.\n\nThe diagram also includes sensory return pathways: vagal afferents projecting to the nucleus of the solitary tract and sympathetic afferents returning toward the thoracic spinal cord. Overall, the image emphasizes the bilateral, highly interconnected control of cardiac function, with sympathetic input generally mobilizing the heart and vagal input providing parasympathetic regulation.",
